export const body = "<p>Chapter 08: On Optimization</p>\n\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n\n<p>By this point, we’ve established the building blocks of a level, design-wise and art-wise. The play experience should be fun and challenging; the direction should be clear; the environments should be meaningful.</p>\n\n<p>Now is the time for the final piece of the puzzle: your level has to run smoothly. And for it to run smoothly, you need to optimize it.</p>\n\n<p>For a detailed look into the rendering pipeline, check <em>3D Graphics Pipeline</em><em>.</em></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6d4a6e29cd27a66b075e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6d4a6e29cd27a66b075e.webp\" width=\"480\" height=\"250\" style=\"--source-width:480px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Playing an unoptimized level</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f522ec6b1ddeab1a21fd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f522ec6b1ddeab1a21fd.webp\" width=\"498\" height=\"280\" style=\"--source-width:498px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Playing a well-optimized level</p>\n\n<h2>What is optimization?</h2>\n\n<p>Optimization is a process of balancing visual quality and performance of a video game. It involves a variety of techniques that ease the stress on the hardware without hurting the graphics and the play experience.</p>\n\n<p>Frame rates are the most talked-about area of optimization, but they are not the only concern. Things like bugs, glitches, crashes are equally important to address because they can break the gameplay.</p>\n\n<p>Although optimization is not a very fun part of a project, it is integral to the final quality. So, let’s learn the basics and make this process less painful for all of us.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/46facc6e637e0577ceda.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/46facc6e637e0577ceda.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"635\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Textures not loaded in</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/110369418e6cb11d4e3b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/110369418e6cb11d4e3b.webp\" width=\"634\" height=\"352\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Bugged collision</p>\n\n<h2>Why optimize?</h2>\n\n<p>No matter how beautiful the art and how great the gameplay, technical deficits can easily sabotage an experience. Issues like frame drops, bugs, and glitches not only kill the immersion of your game world, but also the break the flow of gameplay. Thus, optimization is a very important task.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/bd0e4531d9b531dc906a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/bd0e4531d9b531dc906a.webp\" width=\"1147\" height=\"645\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6e502b8d42766aed02bd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6e502b8d42766aed02bd.webp\" width=\"635\" height=\"354\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>When to optimize?</h2>\n\n<p>Optimization should be a constant throughout the development of your level. In general, there are two things to keep in mind:</p>\n\n<ol><li><strong>Follow the technical specs and guidelines closely</strong></li><li><strong>Playtest your level routinely on the target hardware</strong></li></ol>\n\n<p>It is recommended that you invite other people to play through your level from time to time. They may run into issues that you don’t (because you know your level better than they do).</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1c9ccb8cdde37c84a759.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1c9ccb8cdde37c84a759.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"813\" style=\"--source-width:534px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5a3b1214d0ff7a7a44c6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5a3b1214d0ff7a7a44c6.webp\" width=\"836\" height=\"334\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Preparing for optimization</h2>\n\n<p>As our pipeline showed before, an important action during pre-production is technical investigation: getting to know the <strong>specifications</strong>, the <strong>hardware</strong>, and the <strong>engine</strong>.</p>\n\n<p>Level artists should collaborate with technical artists to avoid as many technical issues as possible. Although the level artists could handle basic optimization, technical artists should be closely involved from the beginning to the end of the level making process. This collaboration will result in a reduced number of iterations down the line.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7accd525f592e510124e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7accd525f592e510124e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"719\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n\n<h2>Performance budget</h2>\n\n<p>It’s important to keep in mind that a level is more than just an environment. There will be characters and VFX and SFX and physics and many other things. Thus, keeping your props as low-cost as you can, while still maintaining enough visual quality, is integral.</p>\n\n<p>Performance budget refers to the limit of the hardware. If you go over the given budget, the frame rate will fluctuate, load times will be protracted, crashes may occur, etc. For that reason, you should learn your target hardware as thoroughly as possible.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/01baa339f9e983ed6f98.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/01baa339f9e983ed6f98.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/afa7a1697f2d0d568169.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/afa7a1697f2d0d568169.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Hardware</h2>\n\n<h3>Overview</h3>\n\n<p>Below are the 4 main pieces of gaming hardware:</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>CPU</strong> (Central Processing Unit): compile and send graphical data to the GPU</li><li><strong>GPU</strong> (Graphical Processing Unit): receive graphical data from the CPU and execute them</li><li><strong>RAM</strong> (Random-Access Memory): store temporary data to run in-use applications</li><li><strong>Storage</strong> (HDD or SSD): store permanent data; write and read data for the RAM</li></ul>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2fa9beee7e7af06c09ac.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2fa9beee7e7af06c09ac.webp\" width=\"277\" height=\"267\" style=\"--source-width:283px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>CPU</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/96fe8a4e9a9c19496841.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/96fe8a4e9a9c19496841.webp\" width=\"607\" height=\"612\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>GPU</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8539988d4333b10f3735.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8539988d4333b10f3735.webp\" width=\"635\" height=\"459\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>RAM</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e3d43b421867d99ef52f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e3d43b421867d99ef52f.webp\" width=\"607\" height=\"578\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>HDD</em></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<h3>About the storage</h3>\n\n<p>The storage stores permanent data of a game. <em>Permanent</em> means the data won’t be lost when you turn the hardware off.</p>\n\n<p>There are currently two storage types:</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>HDD</strong>: more space, but slower transferring speed</li><li><strong>SSD</strong>: expensive, but faster transferring speed</li></ul>\n\n<p>When you endure a long loading screen in a game, it’s either because there is too much data for the storage to load, or storage is too slow. We’ll get into specific examples later when we talk about level streaming.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4979662bfc5849746f50.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4979662bfc5849746f50.webp\" width=\"585\" height=\"268\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 17\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0cb5751413c1e4cceb76.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0cb5751413c1e4cceb76.webp\" width=\"585\" height=\"326\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 18\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>About the RAM</h3>\n\n<p>When you open a game, the storage has to transfer all data in that game to the RAM (Random Access Memory). Only after the data is stored on the memory can it be processed by the CPU.</p>\n\n<p>As a practical example, we can look at textures. More detailed textures occupy more space on the RAM. Therefore, a system with more RAM can load in more detailed textures, but whether or not those textures can be rendered is dependent on the GPU.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/249ec9bbba793e5958dd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/249ec9bbba793e5958dd.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 19\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Compare the ground textures.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6f598f9dc71eca74e03a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6f598f9dc71eca74e03a.webp\" width=\"632\" height=\"281\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 20\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>About the CPU &amp; GPU</h3>\n\n<p>The CPU is good at general tasks while the GPU is good at rendering graphics. That means, the GPU handles the visuals and leaves other things (such as physics and AI) to the CPU.</p>\n\n<p>However, the GPU cannot generate graphics itself. It needs to be guided by the CPU, which leads us to draw calls.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e3c7a35fa7a6fb623131.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e3c7a35fa7a6fb623131.webp\" width=\"842\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:503px\" alt=\"Illustration 21\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Draw calls</h3>\n\n<p>A draw call is basically a call from the CPU to the GPU to draw an object. This means the CPU has to works ahead of the GPU in order for the game to generate frames consistently. Therefore, when a game has a shaky frame rate, don’t just blame the GPU.</p>\n\n<p><em>Keep in mind that the CPU also handles other things such as physics and AI, so CPU bottlenecking doesn’t necessarily equate to draw calls.</em></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e161c9cc31104d45283c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e161c9cc31104d45283c.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"412\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 22\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/99345545d0229ab190dd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/99345545d0229ab190dd.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"412\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 23\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Inside a draw call is information about textures, states, shaders, rendering objects, buffers, etc. The most important thing to remember is each draw call is required by each material. That means the more unique materials you have, the more draw calls are required, and that can affect performance.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2377f92891b076673800.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2377f92891b076673800.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"541\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 24\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/998c730e8b6239b2a778.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/998c730e8b6239b2a778.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"528\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 25\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Game engine</h2>\n\n<h3>What is it?</h3>\n\n<p>A game engine is an application that provides tools for game making. It is similar to Microsoft Word in this sense: It receives files from other applications, uses the feature set to compile and modify them, and then output a new file. But instead of a document, it outputs a video game.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8ff4c54da426da378738.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8ff4c54da426da378738.webp\" width=\"568\" height=\"320\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 26\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1d5be68ebd09dc08bd24.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1d5be68ebd09dc08bd24.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"550\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 27\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>What does it have?</h3>\n\n<p>A modern game engine often includes a graphics renderer, audio, logic, AI, physics, networking and optimization functions.</p>\n\n<p>While the toolset may be similar across different engines, each of them will often has unique functionality and features. Therefore, you should always learn the chosen engines in advance, especially proprietary engines.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d5500d6c24ec5ca07486.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d5500d6c24ec5ca07486.webp\" width=\"610\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 28\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXTRA </strong></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/47718c568aed547a7404.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/47718c568aed547a7404.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"577\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 29\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Profilers</h3>\n\n<p>Profilers are performance visualizers in game engines. They display the level of usage of the CPU and the GPU (among other things) which allows us to identify bottlenecks in the system.</p>\n\n<p>Common bottlenecks are:</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>CPU bound </strong>— object count, draw call, culling</li><li><strong>GPU bound </strong>— shaders, overdraw, lights</li></ul>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a90a450dad0e8e4d5b93.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a90a450dad0e8e4d5b93.webp\" width=\"528\" height=\"330\" style=\"--source-width:528px\" alt=\"Illustration 30\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5ffe941f5bdbcf19e835.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5ffe941f5bdbcf19e835.webp\" width=\"528\" height=\"263\" style=\"--source-width:528px\" alt=\"Illustration 31\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>PRO-TIP</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Test</strong></p>\n\n<p><em>r.ScreenPercentage</em> 10 can help you quickly identify whether your performance issue is CPU- or GPU-bound.  This command asks the game to render less pixels on screen, so if:</p>\n\n<ul><li>The game runs faster, your GPU is over stressed</li><li>The game doesn’t run faster, your CPU is over stressed</li></ul>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0a330ecc2cc2b246afec.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0a330ecc2cc2b246afec.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"984\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 32\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>Another test method</strong></p>\n\n<p>In Unreal Engine PIE Mode, you can use the \"<strong>stat unit</strong>\" command to detect and display the time spent on the game thread, rendering thread and GPU of the current frame.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Frame Time: </strong>The Time Needed to generate a frame. Since Logic Thread (Game) and the Rendering Thread (Draw) need to synchronize at the end of frame, frame time is often similar to one of the threads.</p>\n\n<p><strong>GPU Time: </strong>Time required  by the graphics card to render the current scene, it needs to synchronized with current frame, so it's basically the same as Frame time.</p>\n\n<p>Determine bottleneck using Frame/Game/Draw Time:<a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ce75af9d6b5868d25cb1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ce75af9d6b5868d25cb1.webp\" width=\"384\" height=\"212\" style=\"--source-width:384px\" alt=\"Illustration 33\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<ul><li>If the frame time is close to Game Thread (CPU)/ Render Thread (GPU):</li></ul>\n\n<p>→ The bottleneck appears in the Game Thread (CPU)/Render Thread (GPU)</p>\n\n<ul><li>If both times are not close</li></ul>\n\n<p>→  The bottleneck appears on the graphics card.</p>\n\n<ul><li>Like the image on the right, now our Frame Time is close to Draw Time, so the bottleneck in the GPU.(Render Thread)</li></ul>\n\n<h3>Entity</h3>\n\n<p>An entity is anything that can be placed into a level, such as a character, a prop, a light, or even a camera.</p>\n\n<p>In essence, a video game is a collection of entities, and the gameplay is how the entities are managed.</p>\n\n<p>Other terms for entity are “actor” and “game object.”</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e8f5f0baed137946fd1c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e8f5f0baed137946fd1c.webp\" width=\"972\" height=\"554\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 34\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/bc60d3490886133445a3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/bc60d3490886133445a3.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"663\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 35\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXTRA</strong> – <strong>Entity Component System</strong></p>\n\n<p>For an entity to have meaning in the game world, it must be accompanied by components. You can think of components as different characteristics attributed to an entity, such as velocity, position, and graphics. These components will define how an entity is updated in the game world.</p>\n\n<p>By mixing and matching different components, game developers can create a variety of entities in their games quickly.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fa4c866d8388c0765262.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fa4c866d8388c0765262.webp\" width=\"693\" height=\"565\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 36\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Level streaming</h3>\n\n<p>Level streaming refers to how your level is loaded and displayed on screen for the player. In the opposite pictures, you can see that the hardware only streams in a certain amount of tiles around the player. It also has to unload tiles that are no longer in use to save performance.</p>\n\n<p>The visible result of level streaming is loading times. Generally, the streaming speed is bound the storage (HDD or SSD) of your operating system.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7cdd40f0c8d481835512.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7cdd40f0c8d481835512.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"702\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 37\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b8d72e62416d766aaaac.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b8d72e62416d766aaaac.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"859\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 38\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>EXAMPLE</strong><strong> – </strong><strong>Level streaming</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p>Below is the level streaming details from <em>Marvel’s Spider-Man</em>. They also shared that they had to restrict the swinging speed in this game to ensure the PS4 HDD could load assets in efficiently.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a03debfdfbe3e89867aa.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a03debfdfbe3e89867aa.webp\" width=\"365\" height=\"302\" style=\"--source-width:365px\" alt=\"Illustration 39\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a4c508ddfce8fd9c3c11.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a4c508ddfce8fd9c3c11.webp\" width=\"533\" height=\"302\" style=\"--source-width:533px\" alt=\"Illustration 40\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/082e6a43bb9675827cdf.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/082e6a43bb9675827cdf.webp\" width=\"272\" height=\"302\" style=\"--source-width:272px\" alt=\"Illustration 41\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The storage affects level design more significantly than it might appear. In <em>Jedi Fallen Order</em>, there are some narrow gaps that require that player to squeeze through, basically slowing their movement speed so that assets can be streamed in. And in <em>Mass Effect</em>, there are elevator rides that last longer than they should to mask streaming.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1ec48f62fb9f82f210d3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1ec48f62fb9f82f210d3.webp\" width=\"763\" height=\"425\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 42\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0644dba452e4df26e4b5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0644dba452e4df26e4b5.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"719\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 43\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>The issue previously seen in <em>Jedi Fallen Order</em> is called “pop-in.” It happens when a game can’t load assets (meshes and textures) into the game world fast enough. As a result, things pop in and out of frames gradually. This issue is partly related to Level of Detail and Mipmap, which will be discussed later.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c2e398ce280cc2ac4356.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c2e398ce280cc2ac4356.webp\" width=\"641\" height=\"355\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 44\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e59d0f46fdc2fb814853.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e59d0f46fdc2fb814853.webp\" width=\"641\" height=\"355\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 45\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>There is a level taking place on a train in <em>Medal of Honor: Above and Beyond</em>. The player goes in and out of it to engage in different combat scenarios while their surroundings are constantly changing. To maintain a consistent 90 FPS, our team divided the level into parts and made the environment move along with the camera.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3ab8ce5798c441017d0a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3ab8ce5798c441017d0a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 46\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Visibility culling</h2>\n\n<p>By cutting what is not visible to the player, we can reduce the number of things the hardware has to render, which in turn increases performance. Visibility culling is the specific technique for that purpose.</p>\n\n<p>There are 3 common ways to handle visibility culling:</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>Distance culling</strong></li><li><strong>Frustum culling</strong></li><li><strong>Occlusion culling</strong></li></ul>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/72b529debee12ec15ba6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/72b529debee12ec15ba6.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"832\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 47\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Frustum culling</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e4186f4cb1149ecd46ed.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e4186f4cb1149ecd46ed.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"831\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 48\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Occlusion culling</p>\n\n<h3>Distance culling</h3>\n\n<p>Distance culling is an option to cull geometry (and entities) at certain distance from the camera.</p>\n\n<p>In Unreal Engine 4, <strong>Cull Distance Volumes </strong>are a useful optimization tool that defines what distance to draw (or make visible) any entity within the volume.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/49027f9b31107d11986b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/49027f9b31107d11986b.webp\" width=\"700\" height=\"394\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 49\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/28f9684581166aef1304.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/28f9684581166aef1304.webp\" width=\"765\" height=\"427\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 50\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Frustum culling</h3>\n\n<p>In 3D graphics, frustum refers to the visible region on the screen. Following that logic, frustum culling basically hides everything outside the visible region (the player’s view).</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ac73ce29fbe1ba158b78.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ac73ce29fbe1ba158b78.webp\" width=\"641\" height=\"231\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 51\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3364b67992c8c143252c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3364b67992c8c143252c.webp\" width=\"800\" height=\"450\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 52\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Occlusion culling</h3>\n\n<p>Occlusion culling checks the visibility of all entities within the frustum to see whether they are being blocked (occluded) by something else.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c1acdcb5c198909d044b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c1acdcb5c198909d044b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"809\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 53\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/28f9684581166aef1304.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/28f9684581166aef1304.webp\" width=\"765\" height=\"427\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 54\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Optimization techniques</h2>\n\n<h3>Overview</h3>\n\n<p>Ultimately, optimization is about removing as much stress as possible without sacrificing the overall quality. In this section, we will go through common performance issues and how to address them as a level artist.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/75172f763ad57eb51457.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/75172f763ad57eb51457.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 55\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Batching</h3>\n\n<p>Nowadays, video games feature huge worlds with million upon million of details. If we render entities one by one with separate draw calls, the GPU may  not be able to keep up. Besides, for simple scenes, that approach is not efficient performance-wise.</p>\n\n<p>To solve that issue, we can use batch rendering. Batch rendering is a technique that combine multiple draw calls in a batch to draw them together. With batching, we treat a group of entities as a single piece of geometry so that we can render it more efficiently.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/428bdfd5c59b4a7c921a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/428bdfd5c59b4a7c921a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"756\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 56\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3239d643a64af13df91b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3239d643a64af13df91b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"547\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 57\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>GPU instancing</h3>\n\n<p>Another way to reduce the entity count is <em>instancing</em>. This feature allows us to instantiate identical objects using only one draw call.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4c111b9007bcafa94b5d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4c111b9007bcafa94b5d.webp\" width=\"455\" height=\"327\" style=\"--source-width:455px\" alt=\"Illustration 58\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2f16aaf0e1f29a334e59.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2f16aaf0e1f29a334e59.webp\" width=\"455\" height=\"267\" style=\"--source-width:455px\" alt=\"Illustration 59\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Level of detail</h3>\n\n<p>Level of detail (LOD) is a rendering technique that alters the complexity of a mesh according to its proximity to the player’s view. The further away from the player, the less detailed a mesh becomes. By dynamically swapping between different LODs, video games can present vast, sweeping landscapes without dropping frames.</p>\n\n<p>The pop-in issue we learned earlier was basically due to the storage being unable to timely swap LODs (or swap to an LOD model that is significantly less detailed).</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/dc51594b365869dfd80e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/dc51594b365869dfd80e.webp\" width=\"834\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:499px\" alt=\"Illustration 60\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Mipmaps</h3>\n\n<p>Mipmaps can be considered LODs for textures. The basic principle remains similar: the farther away from the player’s view, the less detailed the textures become.</p>\n\n<p>Similar to LODs, mipmaps can also cause pop-ins.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3b4231a50e22a9808969.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3b4231a50e22a9808969.webp\" width=\"556\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:556px\" alt=\"Illustration 61\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5ba6e0710b813cb6532e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5ba6e0710b813cb6532e.webp\" width=\"556\" height=\"270\" style=\"--source-width:556px\" alt=\"Illustration 62\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Block compression</h3>\n\n<p>Texture compression helps reduce memory load on the RAM, and thus more textures can be loaded. Block compression (BC) is one of the compression techniques.</p>\n\n<p>This technique cuts a texture into small 4x4 blocks. Each block is smaller in size and, thus, quicker to process.</p>\n\n<p>As you can see in the first picture, the color variation of each block is minimal. Therefore, the BC1 format just simplifies them using a four-color palette. The results are nearly identical.</p>\n\n<p>There are 7 different formats of BC, numbered from 1 – 7, each of which has distinct uses.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b39afc3f16781b26bc77.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b39afc3f16781b26bc77.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"481\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 63\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>PRO-TIP</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Block compression formats</strong></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b39afc3f16781b26bc77.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b39afc3f16781b26bc77.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"481\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 64\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Overdraw</h3>\n\n<p>Overdraw is <strong>when a pixel is re-rendered </strong><strong>over and over again</strong>. In the bottom picture, you can see an overdraw visualization of the scene. The bar below displays which area is getting re-rendered the most toward the right side.</p>\n\n<p>As you can see, the smoke form the volcano is re-rendered many times. But this is unavoidable because translucent and transparent materials are see-through, which means the GPU has to render multiple layers of pixels to make them look accurate.</p>\n\n<p>The key takeaway is you should be careful with translucent/transparent objects.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/08eb62f4b22276ef7592.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/08eb62f4b22276ef7592.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"579\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 65\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/28f2dc268cc1ee3ccbd2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/28f2dc268cc1ee3ccbd2.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"579\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 66\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>PRO-TIP</strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Tips for dealing with translucency</strong></p>\n\n<ul><li>Keep most translucent materials unlit</li><li>If a translucent material has to be lit, keep it simple</li><li>Use Additive Blend Mode when you can, it only takes pixels of Material and adds them to the background, so it can save computation time. (It’s similar to Modulate Blend Mode, except it multiplies the value against the pixel of background)</li><li>If you are tight on budget, complicated lit translucent material should only for limited use</li></ul>\n\n<p>Lit translucent materiasl sure look a bit better, but in some scenarios there won’t be much difference, so you have to make sure it’s worth the cost.</p>\n\n<p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8dfdc3109484de221eac.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8dfdc3109484de221eac.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"963\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 67\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n\n<p><strong>Overdraw fix for foliage</strong></p>\n\n<p>Minimize empty masked space as much as possible when you work on foliage.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0731d5b0dd8e7c5f79cc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0731d5b0dd8e7c5f79cc.webp\" width=\"999\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 68\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Overshading</h3>\n\n<p>The GPU doesn’t render pixels individually; instead, it renders pixels in a 2x2 pattern (a quad). So, when a triangle is so small that it only occupies 1 pixel of a quad, the GPU still processes the other 3 empty pixels, leading to 75% wasted work. This situation is called “overshading.”</p>\n\n<p>One way to avoid overshading is to get rid of small, thin triangles—try to keep them big and even. Besides, LODs and culling also help. Still, this can’t be completely avoided since the farther away from an object, the smaller the triangles become.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e2a437c4ff0cf8aa86a2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e2a437c4ff0cf8aa86a2.webp\" width=\"844\" height=\"681\" style=\"--source-width:368px\" alt=\"Illustration 69\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f0aec5856aec52382099.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f0aec5856aec52382099.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"927\" style=\"--source-width:369px\" alt=\"Illustration 70\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Lighting</h3>\n\n<p>A checklist for lighting optimization:</p>\n\n<ul><li>Amount of lights</li><li>Light types: static &lt; stationary &lt; dynamic (most expensive)</li><li>Light cover range</li><li>Light culling distance</li><li>Light shadow number (some lights don’t need to cast shadow)</li><li>Light shadow culling distance</li><li>Reflection probe number</li><li>Reflection probe culling distance</li><li>Volumetric light number (set volumetric intensity to 0 if the light doesn’t need to cast volume)</li><li>Post-processing effects (SSAO, SSR, motion blur)</li><li>Open GPU visualizer to check the lighting related points</li></ul>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2048754f4b701cfbf93b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2048754f4b701cfbf93b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"570\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 71\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/576cd76eeda8d885e362.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/576cd76eeda8d885e362.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"583\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 72\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Only guidelines, not rules</h2>\n\n<p>One final piece of advice before we conclude is, everything you just learned is just a set of guidelines, rather than a set of rules. Real productions will likely differ drastically, and there are as many ways to build levels as there are people who build them.</p>\n\n<p>This isn’t to say you should disregard all the information in here, far from it. This is to say, this document should be used as a reference point—a head start for further study—rather than an all-encompassing answer to every problem you may face in level creation. Every game is different, and thus should be made differently.</p>\n\n<p>With that in mind, let’s revisit the definition of level art.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/554a2b587a02f352e0cc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/554a2b587a02f352e0cc.webp\" width=\"1005\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 73\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Level art in a nutshell</h2>\n\n<p>Level art is the process of transforming the designer’s blockout into a well-realized and believable space. That transformation is partly driven by the concept art.</p>\n\n<p>A skilled level artist not only knows how to make great art and infuse story into the game world, but also how to keep their level run optimally.</p>\n\n<p>Last but not least, a good level artist is a good collaborator.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9dcd804ce5141dc36b4e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9dcd804ce5141dc36b4e.webp\" width=\"629\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:376px\" alt=\"Illustration 74\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Be a good collaborator</h2>\n\n<p>Level designers come up with gameplay ideas. Level artists then turn their ideas into beautiful and meaningful worlds, following the visual guidance of concept artists. Sometimes, design informs art; other times, art informs design. But the best of times are when design and art work in harmony.</p>\n\n<p>The key takeaway is: <strong>Talk to your level designer and concept artist</strong>. Try to understand them and let them understand you. It is mutual understanding that enables us to make games better, together.</p>\n\n<p>Now that you have learned all the fundamentals, I hope you are ready to build some levels, and I look forward to playing them. J</p>\n\n<p>Thank you for reading and happy leveling!</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d4626dd74201c0b09cb7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d4626dd74201c0b09cb7.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 75\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4196243e520982112bc2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4196243e520982112bc2.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"684\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 76\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>";
